      "writtenOn": "2026-10-28T22:02:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agreed that the breakpoint has to go into the slice that the PTE maps, but as I mentioned for the futex key on aosp/4367952, I think `vma_folio_offset(vma, insn_vaddr)` already gives that offset. As far as I can see, every PTE these paths reach in a 4KB VMA was built with `vma_pte_add_slice()`: page cache text and anonymous pages at fault time (aosp/4366901), their COW copies (aosp/4366901, aosp/4368036), and swapped or migrated pages (aosp/4312507). So it maps the slice that `vma_slice_offset()` gives its address.\n\nIf so, would it work to keep `get_user_pages_remote()` and `get_user_pages()` and only replace the in-page offsets with `vma_folio_offset()`, here and in `uprobe_write()`, `__update_ref_ctr()` and `is_trap_at_addr()`? Their callers already have the VMA (`rc_vma` in `update_ref_ctr()`, `vma` in `delayed_ref_ctr_inc()` and in `find_active_uprobe_rcu()`), and for native VMAs `vma_folio_offset()` is the offset in the kernel page, so no `mm_is_p3s_4k()` branches are needed.\n\nNative processes would then keep their GUP calls (with this patch, `__update_ref_ctr()` and `is_trap_at_addr()` go through `get_user_pages_range_locked()` for every mm, and `is_trap_at_addr()` moves from `get_user_pages()` to `get_user_pages_remote()`). `get_user_pages_range_locked()`, which aosp/4367611 now adds for this caller, wouldn\u0027t be needed either, and the patch wouldn\u0027t depend on the per-PTE offset capture in `follow_page_pte()` that I asked about on aosp/4367611.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "8665129a6c8af58a58334fa98b9d1d0a4998349e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
